1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:01,603
It's eleven at night.

2
00:00:01,643 --> 00:00:03,326
Tomorrow is the big meeting...

3
00:00:03,366 --> 00:00:04,557
or the test results...

4
00:00:04,597 --> 00:00:07,081
or the conversation you've been dreading.

5
00:00:07,121 --> 00:00:09,235
And you're lying there, running it.

6
00:00:09,275 --> 00:00:11,389
What if they ask about the numbers?

7
00:00:11,429 --> 00:00:12,744
What if it's bad news?

8
00:00:12,784 --> 00:00:14,180
What if I freeze?

9
00:00:15,230 --> 00:00:17,542
It's exhausting. But it also feels...

10
00:00:17,582 --> 00:00:20,162
responsible. Like you're working on it.

11
00:00:20,202 --> 00:00:23,185
Like if you stopped, something bad would slip

12
00:00:23,225 --> 00:00:24,113
through.

13
00:00:25,163 --> 00:00:26,983
Here's the strange part.

14
00:00:27,023 --> 00:00:30,548
In one study, people tracked their worries for

15
00:00:30,588 --> 00:00:34,035
a month… and about nine out of ten never came

16
00:00:34,075 --> 00:00:37,913
true. So why does worry still feel so useful?

17
00:00:38,963 --> 00:00:41,129
It isn't because you're bad at thinking.

18
00:00:41,169 --> 00:00:43,444
Psychologists have found that worry really

19
00:00:43,484 --> 00:00:44,878
does do something for you.

20
00:00:44,918 --> 00:00:46,532
Just… not the thing you think.

21
00:00:46,572 --> 00:00:48,848
By the end, you'll know what it's actually

22
00:00:48,888 --> 00:00:51,113
doing, and three things that help.

23
00:00:56,963 --> 00:00:59,537
In the early nineteen eighties, a psychologist

24
00:00:59,577 --> 00:01:01,981
at Penn State named Thomas Borkovec started

25
00:01:02,021 --> 00:01:03,913
studying people who worried a lot.

26
00:01:03,953 --> 00:01:06,414
And his team gave worry a definition that is

27
00:01:06,454 --> 00:01:07,770
still used today.

28
00:01:08,820 --> 00:01:11,742
Worry, they wrote, is a chain of thoughts and

29
00:01:11,782 --> 00:01:14,178
images… loaded with negative feeling...

30
00:01:14,218 --> 00:01:16,218
and relatively hard to control.

31
00:01:16,258 --> 00:01:18,983
It's an attempt to solve a problem in your

32
00:01:19,023 --> 00:01:21,813
head. A problem whose outcome is uncertain...

33
00:01:21,853 --> 00:01:23,322
and might be bad.

34
00:01:24,372 --> 00:01:25,851
Notice the key phrase.

35
00:01:25,891 --> 00:01:27,922
An attempt to solve a problem.

36
00:01:27,962 --> 00:01:30,200
That's why worry feels like work.

37
00:01:30,240 --> 00:01:33,100
It runs on the same machinery as planning.

38
00:01:33,140 --> 00:01:36,275
You scan the future, you spot the dangers, you

39
00:01:36,315 --> 00:01:38,322
rehearse what you'd say.

40
00:01:39,372 --> 00:01:40,971
But there's a difference.

41
00:01:41,011 --> 00:01:43,724
Planning ends. You find the next step, you

42
00:01:43,764 --> 00:01:45,887
take it, and the thought lets go.

43
00:01:45,927 --> 00:01:48,705
Worry doesn't end. It reaches the last what

44
00:01:48,745 --> 00:01:51,455
if… and loops back to the first one.

45
00:01:52,505 --> 00:01:54,409
Researchers found a clue about why.

46
00:01:54,449 --> 00:01:56,853
When people describe the problems they worry

47
00:01:56,893 --> 00:01:59,241
about, their descriptions tend to be vaguer

48
00:01:59,281 --> 00:02:01,519
and more abstract than when they describe

49
00:02:01,559 --> 00:02:03,296
problems they don't worry about.

50
00:02:03,336 --> 00:02:04,740
What if it all goes wrong?

51
00:02:04,780 --> 00:02:06,851
Instead of… what exactly would I do on

52
00:02:06,891 --> 00:02:07,685
Tuesday?

53
00:02:08,735 --> 00:02:11,743
That idea is called the reduced concreteness

54
00:02:11,783 --> 00:02:14,653
theory, from Joachim Stoeber and Borkovec.

55
00:02:14,693 --> 00:02:17,562
It isn't settled. Some later studies found

56
00:02:17,602 --> 00:02:20,195
worry is more concrete than the theory

57
00:02:20,235 --> 00:02:23,243
predicts. But it fits what many people feel.

58
00:02:23,283 --> 00:02:25,599
A vague problem has no first step.

59
00:02:25,639 --> 00:02:27,859
So the mind keeps circling.

60
00:02:28,909 --> 00:02:31,507
So if worry so rarely solves anything...

61
00:02:31,547 --> 00:02:33,173
why do we keep doing it?

62
00:02:33,213 --> 00:02:35,671
Borkovec decided to ask the worriers

63
00:02:35,711 --> 00:02:36,825
themselves.

64
00:02:40,475 --> 00:02:42,930
In a study published in nineteen ninety-five

65
00:02:42,970 --> 00:02:45,204
Borkovec and Lizabeth Roemer gave college

66
00:02:45,244 --> 00:02:47,644
students who worried a lot a list of reasons

67
00:02:47,684 --> 00:02:49,030
people give for worrying...

68
00:02:49,070 --> 00:02:51,414
and asked them to rate how typical each one

69
00:02:51,454 --> 00:02:52,525
was for them.

70
00:02:53,575 --> 00:02:56,170
The reasons will probably sound familiar.

71
00:02:56,210 --> 00:02:58,611
Worry motivates me to get things done.

72
00:02:58,651 --> 00:03:00,989
Worry helps me prepare for the worst.

73
00:03:01,029 --> 00:03:03,881
Worry helps me avoid bad outcomes, or prevent

74
00:03:03,921 --> 00:03:04,592
them.

75
00:03:05,642 --> 00:03:07,696
And one that's a little more magical.

76
00:03:07,736 --> 00:03:10,242
Worrying about something makes it less likely

77
00:03:10,282 --> 00:03:12,788
to happen. As if the worry itself were a kind

78
00:03:12,828 --> 00:03:13,970
of protection.

79
00:03:15,020 --> 00:03:16,026
There was one more.

80
00:03:16,066 --> 00:03:18,558
Worry distracts me from more emotional things.

81
00:03:18,598 --> 00:03:21,090
And the students whose worry was strong enough

82
00:03:21,130 --> 00:03:23,511
to meet the criteria for generalized anxiety

83
00:03:23,551 --> 00:03:25,273
disorder… stood out on that one.

84
00:03:25,313 --> 00:03:27,584
They rated it higher than the other groups

85
00:03:27,624 --> 00:03:29,070
did. Keep that one in mind.

86
00:03:29,110 --> 00:03:30,231
It comes back.

87
00:03:31,281 --> 00:03:33,112
Now here's where it gets tricky.

88
00:03:33,152 --> 00:03:35,393
Most of the time, the bad thing doesn't

89
00:03:35,433 --> 00:03:36,738
happen. So you worried...

90
00:03:36,778 --> 00:03:38,259
and then nothing happened.

91
00:03:38,299 --> 00:03:40,247
And your brain draws a conclusion.

92
00:03:40,287 --> 00:03:41,631
The worry worked.

93
00:03:42,681 --> 00:03:45,013
It's like a rain dance in a place where it

94
00:03:45,053 --> 00:03:47,498
rains anyway. Every time the rain comes, the

95
00:03:47,538 --> 00:03:48,741
dance gets the credit.

96
00:03:48,781 --> 00:03:51,282
Researchers call this a superstitious kind of

97
00:03:51,322 --> 00:03:53,880
avoidance. The relief you feel when the feared

98
00:03:53,920 --> 00:03:56,252
thing doesn't happen rewards the worrying...

99
00:03:56,292 --> 00:03:58,280
so you do it again next time.

100
00:03:59,330 --> 00:04:02,429
But how often does the feared thing really not

101
00:04:02,469 --> 00:04:05,158
happen? A few years ago, someone finally

102
00:04:05,198 --> 00:04:06,094
counted.

103
00:04:09,744 --> 00:04:12,564
In a study published in twenty twenty, Lucas

104
00:04:12,604 --> 00:04:15,423
LaFreniere and Michelle Newman, also at Penn

105
00:04:15,463 --> 00:04:18,283
State, worked with twenty-nine students with

106
00:04:18,323 --> 00:04:20,168
generalized anxiety disorder...

107
00:04:20,208 --> 00:04:23,092
a pattern where worry is constant and hard to

108
00:04:23,132 --> 00:04:24,197
switch off.

109
00:04:25,247 --> 00:04:27,928
For ten days, they were prompted four times a

110
00:04:27,968 --> 00:04:30,408
day to write down what they were worrying

111
00:04:30,448 --> 00:04:33,069
about… and what they predicted would happen.

112
00:04:33,109 --> 00:04:35,246
Then, for thirty days, they checked.

113
00:04:35,286 --> 00:04:36,664
Did it come true?

114
00:04:37,714 --> 00:04:40,730
About ninety-one percent of the worries did

115
00:04:40,770 --> 00:04:43,785
not come true. The most common result for a

116
00:04:43,825 --> 00:04:46,485
single person was one hundred percent.

117
00:04:46,525 --> 00:04:49,931
For seven of them… not one worry came true.

118
00:04:50,981 --> 00:04:53,814
And even among the worries that did come true

119
00:04:53,854 --> 00:04:56,625
about three in ten turned out better than the

120
00:04:56,665 --> 00:04:58,264
person had expected.

121
00:04:59,314 --> 00:05:02,220
Before we lean on that number too hard, the

122
00:05:02,260 --> 00:05:04,755
honest caveats. It was a small study.

123
00:05:04,795 --> 00:05:07,289
Mostly young, white, female students.

124
00:05:07,329 --> 00:05:10,098
And it only tracked worries that could be

125
00:05:10,138 --> 00:05:12,064
checked within a month.

126
00:05:13,114 --> 00:05:14,505
But it shows the gap.

127
00:05:14,545 --> 00:05:16,616
The worry felt like a forecast.

128
00:05:16,656 --> 00:05:18,524
It was mostly a false alarm.

129
00:05:18,564 --> 00:05:21,521
And the students whose worries came true the

130
00:05:21,561 --> 00:05:25,045
least… improved the most during the treatment.

131
00:05:26,095 --> 00:05:28,313
Which raises a bigger question.

132
00:05:28,353 --> 00:05:31,517
If worry predicts so badly, maybe predicting

133
00:05:31,557 --> 00:05:32,900
isn't its real job.

134
00:05:32,940 --> 00:05:35,912
Maybe worry is doing something else.

135
00:05:39,562 --> 00:05:42,136
Back to Borkovec. In nineteen ninety, he and a

136
00:05:42,176 --> 00:05:44,522
colleague, Shao Hu, ran an experiment with

137
00:05:44,562 --> 00:05:47,298
people who were afraid of public speaking.

138
00:05:48,348 --> 00:05:51,095
Before the test, each person spent a short

139
00:05:51,135 --> 00:05:52,954
time in one of three states.

140
00:05:52,994 --> 00:05:55,608
Some relaxed. Some thought about neutral

141
00:05:55,648 --> 00:05:57,268
things. And some worried.

142
00:05:57,308 --> 00:06:00,254
Then they imagined giving a speech, again and

143
00:06:00,294 --> 00:06:03,498
again, while their heart rate was measured.

144
00:06:04,548 --> 00:06:07,405
You'd expect the worriers to react the most.

145
00:06:07,445 --> 00:06:10,434
They didn't. Their heart rate jumped the least

146
00:06:10,474 --> 00:06:12,212
when the speech scene came.

147
00:06:12,252 --> 00:06:15,175
But… they reported more fear than the neutral

148
00:06:15,215 --> 00:06:16,618
group, not less.

149
00:06:17,668 --> 00:06:18,981
Borkovec's explanation.

150
00:06:19,021 --> 00:06:20,276
Worry is mostly words.

151
00:06:20,316 --> 00:06:22,747
It's talking to yourself about danger, not

152
00:06:22,787 --> 00:06:25,219
picturing it. And that inner talk seems to

153
00:06:25,259 --> 00:06:26,513
mute the body's alarm.

154
00:06:26,553 --> 00:06:27,925
Which feels like relief...

155
00:06:27,965 --> 00:06:30,573
but it may also keep the fear from ever being

156
00:06:30,613 --> 00:06:31,927
fully felt, and fading.

157
00:06:31,967 --> 00:06:34,633
Remember the students who said worry distracts

158
00:06:34,673 --> 00:06:36,516
them from more emotional things?

159
00:06:36,556 --> 00:06:38,907
This is what that might look like.

160
00:06:39,957 --> 00:06:42,794
Michelle Newman and Sandra Llera took the idea

161
00:06:42,834 --> 00:06:45,670
a step further. Their model is called contrast

162
00:06:45,710 --> 00:06:48,422
avoidance. It says that what many people who

163
00:06:48,462 --> 00:06:50,923
worry a lot fear most isn't only the bad

164
00:06:50,963 --> 00:06:52,674
event. It's the sudden drop...

165
00:06:52,714 --> 00:06:55,253
from feeling okay to feeling awful.

166
00:06:56,303 --> 00:06:58,765
In one of their experiments, people worried

167
00:06:58,805 --> 00:07:00,868
relaxed, or thought neutral thoughts...

168
00:07:00,908 --> 00:07:03,029
and then watched upsetting film clips.

169
00:07:03,069 --> 00:07:05,417
Those who had worried first felt less of a

170
00:07:05,457 --> 00:07:07,805
jump when the frightening or sad clip hit.

171
00:07:07,845 --> 00:07:09,454
They were already down there.

172
00:07:09,494 --> 00:07:11,663
There was less distance to fall.

173
00:07:12,713 --> 00:07:15,058
And here's the part that surprised them.

174
00:07:15,098 --> 00:07:17,682
When people with generalized anxiety relaxed

175
00:07:17,722 --> 00:07:20,246
first, they were more likely than others to

176
00:07:20,286 --> 00:07:22,929
say the sudden switch into the upsetting clip

177
00:07:22,969 --> 00:07:25,493
felt hard to bear. Calm, for them, made the

178
00:07:25,533 --> 00:07:26,837
drop feel worse.

179
00:07:27,887 --> 00:07:30,836
Think of it like riding with one foot on the

180
00:07:30,876 --> 00:07:33,824
brake the whole time, so a sudden stop never

181
00:07:33,864 --> 00:07:35,862
jolts you. It works, in a way.

182
00:07:35,902 --> 00:07:38,375
But you never get to enjoy the drive.

183
00:07:38,415 --> 00:07:41,499
And when nothing bad happens, you've spent the

184
00:07:41,539 --> 00:07:42,858
whole evening tense...

185
00:07:42,898 --> 00:07:44,063
for nothing.

186
00:07:45,113 --> 00:07:47,992
To be fair, contrast avoidance is a model

187
00:07:48,032 --> 00:07:51,053
that's still being tested, mostly in people

188
00:07:51,093 --> 00:07:54,256
with generalized anxiety, and mostly in labs.

189
00:07:54,296 --> 00:07:56,819
But it explains why worry feels like

190
00:07:56,859 --> 00:07:58,955
protection. In a narrow sense...

191
00:07:58,995 --> 00:07:59,772
it is.

192
00:08:03,422 --> 00:08:04,709
So is all worry useless?

193
00:08:04,749 --> 00:08:07,087
Not quite. In twenty seventeen, Kate Sweeny

194
00:08:07,127 --> 00:08:09,520
and Michael Dooley reviewed the research and

195
00:08:09,560 --> 00:08:10,570
found real upsides.

196
00:08:10,610 --> 00:08:12,782
Moderate worry is linked to people doing

197
00:08:12,822 --> 00:08:14,994
protective things… like getting a health

198
00:08:15,034 --> 00:08:17,153
screening, or wearing sunscreen.

199
00:08:18,203 --> 00:08:21,031
The difference seems to be where the worry

200
00:08:21,071 --> 00:08:24,172
goes. Useful worry ends with something you do.

201
00:08:24,212 --> 00:08:25,948
You worry about your skin...

202
00:08:25,988 --> 00:08:27,450
you buy the sunscreen...

203
00:08:27,490 --> 00:08:29,089
and the thought lets go.

204
00:08:29,129 --> 00:08:30,864
Useless worry has no exit.

205
00:08:30,904 --> 00:08:32,347
It just circles.

206
00:08:33,397 --> 00:08:36,760
So here are three things researchers have

207
00:08:36,800 --> 00:08:39,665
tested. Information, not treatment.

208
00:08:39,705 --> 00:08:41,657
First… make it concrete.

209
00:08:41,697 --> 00:08:45,475
When a worry starts looping, ask one question.

210
00:08:45,515 --> 00:08:49,128
What is the next physical step I could take?

211
00:08:49,168 --> 00:08:52,780
If there is one, write it down and give it a

212
00:08:52,820 --> 00:08:54,523
time. If there isn't...

213
00:08:54,563 --> 00:08:57,594
that's a sign this loop isn't solving

214
00:08:57,634 --> 00:08:58,731
anything.

215
00:08:59,781 --> 00:09:01,883
Second… give worry an appointment.

216
00:09:01,923 --> 00:09:04,277
This goes back to Borkovec in nineteen

217
00:09:04,317 --> 00:09:07,049
eighty-three. Pick a set time and place, say

218
00:09:07,089 --> 00:09:09,317
thirty minutes in the early evening.

219
00:09:09,357 --> 00:09:12,089
When a worry shows up during the day, jot it

220
00:09:12,129 --> 00:09:14,684
down, and postpone it to that slot.

221
00:09:15,734 --> 00:09:18,533
A trial published in twenty twenty-four found

222
00:09:18,573 --> 00:09:21,371
that worry dropped in people with generalized

223
00:09:21,411 --> 00:09:24,146
anxiety after less than a week of postponing

224
00:09:24,186 --> 00:09:26,039
it. But the evidence is mixed.

225
00:09:26,079 --> 00:09:28,310
Another study found no clear effect.

226
00:09:28,350 --> 00:09:31,475
So treat it as an experiment, not a promise.

227
00:09:32,525 --> 00:09:35,287
Third… keep score. Write the worry down as a

228
00:09:35,327 --> 00:09:36,816
prediction, with a date.

229
00:09:36,856 --> 00:09:38,090
Then check it later.

230
00:09:38,130 --> 00:09:40,892
That's exactly what the students in the Penn

231
00:09:40,932 --> 00:09:43,822
State study did. Seeing your own false alarms

232
00:09:43,862 --> 00:09:46,434
in your own handwriting, is hard to argue

233
00:09:46,474 --> 00:09:47,142
with.

234
00:09:48,192 --> 00:09:51,203
And if worry is taking over your days, your

235
00:09:51,243 --> 00:09:54,466
sleep, or your health, that's not something to

236
00:09:54,506 --> 00:09:57,233
fix with a video. Talk to a doctor or a

237
00:09:57,273 --> 00:09:58,581
licensed therapist.

238
00:09:58,621 --> 00:10:00,674
Worry is very treatable.

239
00:10:01,724 --> 00:10:03,812
So, back to eleven at night.

240
00:10:03,852 --> 00:10:05,637
The meeting is tomorrow.

241
00:10:05,677 --> 00:10:08,297
Your brain is running the what ifs.

242
00:10:08,337 --> 00:10:10,502
Now you know what it's doing.

243
00:10:10,542 --> 00:10:12,858
It isn't predicting the future.

244
00:10:12,898 --> 00:10:16,051
It's bracing you… keeping you a little bit

245
00:10:16,091 --> 00:10:19,025
down, so a fall won't feel so far.

246
00:10:20,075 --> 00:10:21,832
You can thank it for trying.

247
00:10:21,872 --> 00:10:23,693
And then ask it one question.

248
00:10:23,733 --> 00:10:25,040
What's the next step?

249
00:10:25,080 --> 00:10:27,030
If there is one, write it down.

250
00:10:27,070 --> 00:10:29,725
If there isn't… the worry can wait for its

251
00:10:29,765 --> 00:10:30,885
appointment.

252
00:10:31,935 --> 00:10:33,319
So tell me in the comments.

253
00:10:33,359 --> 00:10:35,693
What's one thing you worried about last month

254
00:10:35,733 --> 00:10:36,748
that never happened?

255
00:10:36,788 --> 00:10:38,858
And if you like your psychology with the

256
00:10:38,898 --> 00:10:39,913
receipts, subscribe.

257
00:10:39,953 --> 00:10:41,885
There's a new page every week.
